  "worked_solution": "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{5{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{5{e}^{-t}}+L{3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =5L{{e}^{-t}}+3L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =5L{{e}^{-t}}+3L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+1}+3\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{18}{(s-2)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$",
